WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.322
Digital signatures appear inside certificates and public key
infrastructure at a high level.

2
00:00:05.322 --> 00:00:09.246
Certificates bind public keys to identities through a chain of trust.

3
00:00:09.246 --> 00:00:17.628
Applications that rely on certificate validation should use standard
libraries and platform validation rather than inventing certificate rules.

4
00:00:17.628 --> 00:00:29.601
The important principle is that signature verification is part of a larger
trust decision about who issued the certificate, which identity it
represents, and whether the certificate is valid for the intended use.

5
00:00:29.601 --> 00:00:31.996
Signed tokens are another common pattern.

6
00:00:31.996 --> 00:00:38.980
A token may contain claims such as subject, issuer, audience, expiration,
scope, role, tenant, or authentication context.

7
00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:45.033
The signature helps detect tampering and establish the issuer, but the
application must validate context.

8
00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:54.745
A signed token from the wrong issuer, wrong audience, expired time window,
or wrong intended use should not be trusted merely because its signature
is mathematically valid.

9
00:00:54.745 --> 00:00:58.470
Webhook and API request signing concepts follow the same pattern.

10
00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:03.259
The receiver verifies that a message came from an expected party and was
not modified.

11
00:01:03.259 --> 00:01:11.508
Context often includes timestamp windows, request identifiers, selected
headers, body bytes, endpoint paths, or nonce concepts at a high level.

12
00:01:11.508 --> 00:01:19.291
These details help prevent replay and confusion, but only if the signer
and verifier agree on what is covered and how freshness is enforced.

13
00:01:19.291 --> 00:01:23.082
Accepting any valid signature from any key is not a safe trust model.

14
00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:31.197
A Python application should define which issuers, keys, algorithms,
audiences, time windows, and purposes are accepted for each integration.

15
00:01:31.197 --> 00:01:35.322
Context validation matters in addition to cryptographic verification.

16
00:01:35.322 --> 00:01:42.040
Signed data can still be stale, malformed, unauthorized, outside business
policy, or intended for a different system.
